WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.910
- Alright y'all, it's
time to scroll through the

00:00:01.910 --> 00:00:03.690
busy and bountiful pages,

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:05.750
bountiful, you can't say that word enough,

00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:07.820
of social media to share something sure

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:09.380
to brighten your day or hopefully it does.

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:10.781
This is what I'm liking.

00:00:10.781 --> 00:00:13.364
(upbeat music)

00:00:14.216 --> 00:00:16.230
On this, the International
Day of the Girl,

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:18.970
I want to tell you about
nine-year-old Ayla.

00:00:18.970 --> 00:00:22.100
Two years ago, she developed
a deep passion for running.

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:24.470
As much as she loved it,
she didn't make her school's

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:26.350
all star cross-country team;

00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:28.380
but she didn't let it knock her down.

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:31.320
Instead, she found another
team in town and worked,

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:34.430
and practiced, and worked, and practiced.

00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:38.277
Now she's a bonafide
record-breaking track star.

00:00:38.277 --> 00:00:40.010
That's the kind of drive that's led

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:42.450
to Mira and Valeria to success.

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:45.610
But with Ayla, it's not even
what makes her extraordinary.

00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:47.770
Ayla was born without her fibula,

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:50.750
and her lower leg was
amputated when she was two.

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:52.670
We have Ayla and her mom, Laura,

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:55.138
on the line right now. What's up y'all?

00:00:55.138 --> 00:00:58.602
(audience cheering)

00:00:58.602 --> 00:01:02.240
Hello! So what is it, Ayla?

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:05.090
What is it about running
that you love so much?

00:01:05.090 --> 00:01:08.920
- I love running with my friend
and I am really competitive

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:10.210
and I love racing.

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:13.990
- That's amazing, Ayla, a great job.

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:17.830
So you're now competing
in huge races, right?

00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:18.663
- Yes.

00:01:19.515 --> 00:01:21.830
- I'll brag on her a little bit.

00:01:21.830 --> 00:01:24.020
She has done some national races

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:27.600
with her club team against typical kids.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:30.563
And she's medaled at some
of these national races.

00:01:30.563 --> 00:01:32.350
And then this past summer,

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:35.350
she decided she wanted
to try adapted racing.

00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:38.381
So she's competing against
kids that are similar to her,

00:01:38.381 --> 00:01:40.990
and she qualified for junior nationals.

00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:43.399
And in July, she went
out to junior nationals,

00:01:43.399 --> 00:01:45.060
won a bunch of gold medals,

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:47.600
and actually broke
three U.S. track records

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:49.687
for her age and disability type.

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:53.247
- That's incredible, Ayla!

00:01:54.410 --> 00:01:56.120
I hope you are so proud

00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:58.870
of yourself. That's an
amazing thing to accomplish.

00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:00.550
So, Ayla, you say that there's

00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:02.550
an important lesson here, right?

00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:04.250
- Yes, the important lesson is

00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:06.070
if you want to try out a sport,

00:02:06.070 --> 00:02:07.770
you're not always going
to like it at first,

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:09.690
because you got to practice,
practice, practice.

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:10.523
If you don't like it,

00:02:10.523 --> 00:02:12.000
you don't have to force yourself to do it.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.200
You can just stop and
find a different sport

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:15.470
that you can try.

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:18.190
- Yeah. I love sports, though.

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:20.532
It teaches you so many things.

00:02:20.532 --> 00:02:22.700
It's more than just the actual sport.

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:24.210
It does teach you a lot of things

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:26.740
mentally, emotionally,
teamwork, all of that.

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:28.280
But I'm so proud of you, Ayla,

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:30.140
congratulations. Thank you, Laura.

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:32.060
Thank y'all for your time.

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:33.867
I'm liking your posts, right now.

00:02:34.873 --> 00:02:35.706
(doorbell ring)

00:02:35.706 --> 00:02:38.200
And that's not all. Our
season long partner,

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:40.500
Pilot pen, the go-to pen
of the Kelly Clarkson Show

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:42.569
wants to honor ultimate overachievers

00:02:42.569 --> 00:02:44.910
just like you, Ayla. So they'll be using

00:02:44.910 --> 00:02:47.750
their G2 gel ink pen to write you a check

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:49.726
for $1,000. How's that sound?

00:02:49.726 --> 00:02:53.080
(audience cheering)

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:55.583
I think Laura got the most excited.

